@RonYanDaik Can you try with [`physics/3d/run_on_separate_thread`](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/classes/class_projectsettings.html#class-projectsettings-property-physics-3d-run-on-separate-thread) set to false? If that doesn't help, then you can also try disabling [`rendering/textures/vram_compression/compress_with_gpu`](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/classes/class_projectsettings.html#class-projectsettings-property-rendering-textures-vram-compression-compress-with-gpu)

As @jrouwe has pointed out, we have a couple of places where we intentionally spin a loop where we yield to the WorkerThreadPool. The idea is that we can pin a system to a specific thread, but still allow other tasks to execute on that thread by yielding to the WTP. This is important for things like rendering and GPU compression which need to always execute on a single thread, but don't need to be on the main thread. 

It seems in this case that Betsy grabbed the same thread that was already used as the Physics thread. Since the loop never returns (it just yields to the WTP), the Physics thread is never able to sync and it causes a deadlock. 

We need a mechanism to ensure that these types of pinned tasks never get assigned to the same thread. In other words, pinned tasks should always be at the top of the callstack. 

To sketch out the process a bit more while it is on my brain, what we do is the following:

```
// System thread init

void System::init() {
	WorkerThreadPool::TaskID tid = WorkerThreadPool::get_singleton()->add_task(callable_mp(this, &System::_thread_loop), true);
	command_queue.set_pump_task_id(tid); // Registers the WTP ID with the command queue
	command_queue.push(this, &System::_assign_mt_ids, tid); // function to save the thread ID locally
	command_queue.push_and_sync(this, &System::_init); // ensure initialized happens on the pinned thread
}
```

This setup ensures that any task that comes through the command_queue will be queued up to execute immediately after the running task on the WTP and will run on the same thread as the WTP task. The glaring error here is that the WTP task is requested like a normal task (albeit high priority) and thus can be allocated onto a thread with a pump task already.

```
// Infinite loop that yields to WTP. The yield is automatically ended once anything is pushed to the command_queue.
void System::_thread_loop() {
	while (!exit) {
		WorkerThreadPool::get_singleton()->yield();
		command_queue.flush_all();
	}
}
```

The assumption in this function is that the work assigned to that thread will return in finite time. If, for example, we pick up another pump task, then the yield will never return and we can never flush.
